{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>Adonai, why have You harmed this people.</b> This Name — אל\"ף דל\"ת — for from the attribute of mercy Moses was not afraid that He would harm the people, but he was afraid of the attribute of judgement, which is the additional ה\"א in the word הרעותה. And he said: Why have You set out to harm this people in the attribute of judgement? Why did You send me before the appointed end had arrived, for it is not fitting for You to send Your emissary before You are willing to deliver them? Thus Ramban wrote." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> ״למה הרעותה לעם הזה״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: Moses, it is a pity for what is lost and cannot be found. I said to Abraham (Genesis 13:17): ״קום התהלך בארץ״ and so forth; he sought a place to bury Sarah and did not find one until he weighed out four hundred silver shekels, and he did not question My ways. I said to Isaac: ״גור בארץ הזאת״ and so forth; he sought water to drink and did not find it, as it says (Genesis 26:20): ״ויריבו רועי גרר״, and he did not question My ways. I said to Jacob: ״הארץ אשר אתה שוכב עליה״ and so forth; he sought a place to pitch his tent and did not find one until he purchased it for a hundred kesitahs, and he did not question My ways. Yet you — at the very beginning of your mission you said to Me, ״What is Your Name?״ and now you say to Me, ״Why have You harmed?״ — ״Now you shall see what I will do to Pharaoh,״ but you will not see the war against the thirty-one kings, which Joshua waged against them. From here you learn that Moses received his sentence, that he would not enter the Land. Thus far." "<b>And it appears to me</b> from the plain sense of Scripture to say that there was no sin in Moses' utterance, ״למה הרעותה,״ and this for two reasons. The first: that he came to inform us of the nature of evil, and that He, blessed be He, is the one who brings good and brings harm, for even evil comes from Him, blessed be He. And this is the matter of the prophet who rebuked those who say (Zephaniah 1:12): ״לא ייטיב ה׳ ולא ירע,״ and it is written (Lamentations 3:37): ״מי זה אמר ותהי״ and so forth, and it is written (ibid.): ״מפי עליון לא תצא״ and so forth. The second: that he went back and repeated it another time, in the portion of the complainers: ״למה הרעות לעבדך,״ and had Moses sensed that the Holy One, blessed be He, reckoned it to him as a sin, he would not have repeated it. Rather, the sin was in what he said: ״ומאז באתי אל פרעה לדבר בשמך הרע לעם הזה״ — that is, that from the time he came before Pharaoh on the mission of the great Name, it went badly for this people, for they did not know Him and he was not humbled before Him at all; and this utterance is like questioning His ways and like flinging words against the Blessed Name — the great Name that He had made known to him in the bush. Therefore He answered him: ״Now you shall see what I will do to Pharaoh,״ by the power of My great Name, which will become known to him, and he will acknowledge it, and all the harm done to the people will be turned back upon him, until he himself admits to sending them forth against his will. And since they interpreted ״Now you shall see״ — and you shall not see what I will do to the thirty-one kings — this constitutes punishment and the taking of the sentence, that he would not enter the Land." "<b>And I found</b> support for my words from what they said in the Midrash on ״ומאז באתי אל פרעה״: Moses said before the Holy One, blessed be He: Master of the universe, with ״אז״ I sinned, with ״אז״ I praise You — with ״אז״ I sinned: ״ומאז באתי אל פרעה״; with ״אז״ I praise You (Exodus 15:1): ״אז ישיר משה.״ And the Midrash I cited above — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: At the beginning of your mission you said to Me, ״What is Your Name?״ and now you say to Me, ״Why have You harmed?״ — the Midrash mentions ״למה הרעותה״ because it is the beginning of the utterances to Moses, not meaning to indicate that this itself is the sin, for the reasons I have stated." "<b>And I saw</b> in the commentary of our Rabbi Hananel, of blessed memory, that he wrote that the expression ״למה הרעותה״ is not the language of complaint and flinging of words, but is the language of inquiry. For Moses asked before the Holy One, blessed be He, regarding this measure: Why is it that the righteous fare ill and the wicked fare well? For he saw Israel in great and terrible servitude and in many troubles, while the Egyptians, the wicked deniers, he saw in prosperity and tranquility. And since sufferings come for several reasons — there are those upon whom sufferings come for sins they have committed, as it is written (Isaiah 57:17): ״בעון בצעי קצפתי ואכהו הסתר ואקצוף,״ and there are those upon whom sufferings come in order to benefit them in their end, as it is written (Deuteronomy 8:16): ״למען ענותך ולמען נסותך להיטיבך באחריתך,״ and these are the sufferings of love, as it is written (Psalms 94:12): ״אשרי הגבר אשר תיסרנו יה״ — therefore, when Moses saw that from the day he came to Pharaoh with the word of the Blessed Name, his yoke was made heavier upon them, and he said: ״תכבד העבודה״ and so forth, he asked the Blessed Name: Why have You set out to harm this people, when the power to deliver them is in Your hand, yet deliver them You did not? And it is like (Exodus 1:17): ״ותחיין את הילדים,״ and likewise: ״וכל הבת תחיון״ — for it is not in human hands to put to death or to bring to life, but the intent is to leave them alive. And so here too: ״למה הרעותה״ — why have You set out to harm — and I am afraid lest He add further harm." "<b>And behold.</b> This question is the question of the prophet Jeremiah, who said (Jeremiah 12:1): ״מדוע דרך רשעים צלחה שלו כל בוגדי בגד״ — he asked of the Holy One, blessed be He, whether the tranquility of the wicked is a reward for kindness they already did in this world, or whether it is in order to multiply their punishment and increase their suffering. And the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him that the tranquility and success of the wicked is nothing but to add punishment upon their punishment, as David said (Psalms 69:28): ״תנה עון על עונם ואל יבאו בצדקתך״. And so here too, the reply of the Holy One, blessed be He, to Moses — ״עתה תראה אשר אעשה״ — means: these sufferings for Israel are nothing but to multiply reward upon their reward; that is the meaning of ״עתה תראה״. And the success and tranquility for Pharaoh is nothing but to multiply punishment upon their punishment; that is the meaning of what is said, ״אשר אעשה לפרעה״ — that is, I have already prepared the plagues for him. And for this reason it was from Me, the cause, that the labor was made heavier upon them from the day I sent you, in order to multiply punishment upon their punishment and to increase and magnify reward for Israel when they stand in those sufferings and endure them with love." "<b>And know</b> that the affairs of this first exile allude to the affairs of this latter exile in which we stand, in servitude and suffering under the dominion of Edom, while we await the salvation of the Holy One, blessed be He — as David, peace be upon him, said concerning this matter (Psalms 130:6): ״נפשי לה׳ משומרים לבקר שומרים לבקר״ — watchmen waiting for the morning, waiting and turning back and waiting again. And if the redemption should be delayed in coming, it is all in order to add good reward for us and to benefit us in our end, and in order to add calamity and punishment to those who subjugate us. And just as we find that the hour pressed hard for Israel in Egypt from the time the first redeemer came before Pharaoh and said to him by the word of HaShem, ״שלח את עמי ויחוגו לי במדבר״, and after this the labor grew heavier upon them and hatred was aroused against them and increased exceedingly beyond what it had been — so too in this future redemption, with the advent of the last redeemer in the exile, hatred will be aroused between the nations and Israel, and they will add servitude upon their servitude. And the redeemer will be revealed and then hidden again, in order to lead the nations astray and to harden their hearts, for so we find in the redemption from Egypt: Moses was revealed to them and then hidden from them. And this is what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: ״ויפגעו את משה ואת אהרן״ — after six months the Holy One, blessed be He, revealed Himself in Midian and said to him: ״לך שוב מצרימה״; Moses came from Midian and Aaron from Egypt, and the officers of Israel encountered them as they were coming out from before Pharaoh. And so they expounded in the Midrash on ״חזית״ (Song of Songs 2:9): ״דומה דודי לצבי״ — just as this gazelle appears and is hidden and appears again, so the first redeemer appeared to them and was hidden from them and appeared to them again. And how long was he hidden from them? Rabbi Tanhuma says: three months — that is what is written: ״ויפגעו את משה ואת אהרן״. Rabbi Yehudah bar Rabbi says: לקריסין, that is to say, the language of encountering is בפרקים, intermittent. And so too the future redeemer will be revealed and then hidden again, for this redemption is destined to be like the model of the Egyptian redemption in many respects. And so the prophet said (Micah 7:15): ״כימי צאתך מארץ מצרים אראנו נפלאות״. And as the End approaches, there will be many and intensifying troubles, and that is a sign of the salvation for Israel. And so they said in the Midrash on (Isaiah 59:19): ״כי יבא כנהר צר רוח ה׳ נוססה בו״ — if you see a generation upon which troubles come like a river, wait for Him, as it is said: ״כי יבא כנהר צר רוח ה׳ נוססה בו״, and adjacent to it (ibid.): ״ובא לציון גואל ולשבי פשע ביעקב נאם ה׳״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {6 "Text reads שומרים לבקר ממתינים וחוזרים וממתינים after the verse quotation from Psalms 130:6, apparently a running explication of the watchman image rather than separate scriptural citation; rendered continuously."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:57:00.464938176Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}